<!--
    decbench campaign case
    O2-noinline-betaflight-betaflight_STM32F405-applyLedFixedLayers (NOVEL
    pool: kuna's graph edit distance on that function already ties the best
    reference decompiler, 25 vs ghidra 25 / angr 42 / ida 41, and the emitted
    body still was not compilable C, so no rival decompiler surfaced this fix).

    P9 emission, declaration emission.  Upstream `PrintC::emitScopeVarDecls`
    walks the ScopeLocal *Symbol* table (printc.cc:2667/2696), so one symbol is
    one declaration by construction.  kuna's printer walks HighVariables
    instead, so a stack slot whose live ranges did not merge was declared once
    per high.  The shared-storage naming gives every whole cover of the slot the
    same name, and the highs recover different types, so the function opened
    with the same identifier declared twice at two widths:

        unsigned int v12; // stack - 0x24
        uint4 v12;        // stack - 0x24

    which is not compilable C and leaves the reader unable to tell which `v12`
    any body occurrence means.  Ghidra emits one `undefined4 local_24;`, IDA one
    `int v23;`.  `option dedupvardecls` (default on) was the only collapse and
    it keys on the fully rendered LINE, so two spellings of one slot both
    survived by construction.

    The fix keys the scalar collapse on the ScopeLocal Symbol, as upstream does:
    highs whose declaration representative resolves to the same containing
    symbol (base-byte containment, the `Funcdata::linkSymbol` query) AND that
    render the same identifier emit one declaration.  Requiring the identifier
    to match is what keeps this from ever removing the last declaration of a
    referenced name.  When the collapsing highs disagree about the type, the
    survivor declares the symbol's own type (upstream `emitVarDecl` declares
    `sym->getType()`, printc.cc:1719) unless that type is narrower than the
    widest storage the group covers, in which case the widest member wins: a
    declaration smaller than the object the body writes through would be a fresh
    bug, not a faithful one.

    No option of its own: this is a correctness fix inside the existing
    default-on `dedupvardecls`, which already owns "collapse duplicate local
    declarations" (DIV-52).

    Bytes: betaflight_STM32F405 (O2-noinline, stripped Cortex-M4 firmware) at
    0x804f5b4, a 72-byte leaf that builds a 5-byte protocol frame across two
    adjacent stack slots and hands their address to a send routine.  It is the
    smallest witness of the same defect in that firmware; the campaign case at
    0x8052b04 is the same shape 20x larger.  Its two callees are stubbed `bx lr`
    at their real addresses.  The firmware is stripped, so the three names here
    are the test's own, read off the p-code: `slot_checksum(&slot,4)` computes a
    byte over the 4-byte slot and `send_bytes(port,&slot,5)` sends the slot plus
    the trailing byte at stack - 0x14.

    The 4-byte slot at stack - 0x18 is exactly the defect: kuna maps only a
    2-byte ScopeLocal symbol there while the body writes it 3 and 4 bytes wide,
    so the two highs render `undefined3` and `unsigned int` and the symbol's own
    type is too narrow to arbitrate.  The widest cover therefore wins.

    Pass 1 (default) asserts the fix: the slot is declared exactly once, at the
    width the body uses.  Pass 2 (`option dedupvardecls off`) pins the
    un-collapsed one-line-per-high rendering, which is the bug.  Assertion
    counts span both passes.

    (kuna GH-181: foldcallret no longer sinks a call past a read of its own
    INDIRECT output, so the checksum call is an explicit statement sequenced
    before the CONCAT13 that reads the slot it may write; its r0 return became
    v1 and renumbered the slot locals to v2/v3.)
-->
